-- title:
--   Interpolation obstruction restricted to local quadratic spectra
-- statement:
--   Retain every hypothesis and all the data of the [interpolation-rigidity frontier](https://prove2.me/theorems/35428579-0ae4-47de-bc13-8f2bb94e6f52). In particular, C is the uniform positive constant, Y is the tested subset, Z consists of its points outside the lattice, N=3U+1 and d=2N|Z|. The auxiliary polynomial, vanishing orders, derivative degree bounds, chart certificates, contact quotient, formal series and all denominator certificates satisfy the parent's assumptions.
--
--   Using the parent's formal series Aₚᵣ,Bₚᵣ,Dᵣ, define the complex coefficients
--
--   $$u_{pr}=[X^0](2A_{pr}),\qquad
--   v_{pr}=[X^0](A_{pr}^2-D_rB_{pr}^2).$$
--
--   Thus qₚᵣ=1−uₚᵣX+vₚᵣX² and Ωₚ=∏ᵣqₚᵣᴺ. Let L be the parent's algebraic closure of the fraction field of ℂ[t], and write t∈L for the image of the polynomial variable. A bar denotes the canonical coefficient extension ℂ→L. For weights w:Z×Fin(N)→L, write
--
--   $$W_{p,j,w}(X)=\sum_{r\in Z}\sum_{k<N}w_{rk}\,\overline V_{prjk}(X).$$
--
--   The assertion is that the same uniform C permits p, a nonconstant polynomial F with polynomial coefficients, and nonnegative integers a,b,s satisfying the parent's coefficient degree bound, contact-quotient relation and unchanged cost bound
--
--   $$2(\deg(F)b+sa)+(U+1)\le Cn^2,$$
--
--   with the following exclusion. For each root λ of F.map(φ), require the exclusion if either s<d or
--
--   $$\lambda^2-\overline u_{pr}\lambda+\overline v_{pr}=0
--   \quad\text{for some }r\in Z.$$
--
--   The exclusion says that no single weight family w satisfies, for every 0≤j≤b, the parent's interpolation condition: divisibility of (1−λX)Wₚ,ⱼ,𝑤−tʲΩ̄ₚ by X^(s+1) when s<d, and the exact identity (1−λX)Wₚ,ⱼ,𝑤=tʲΩ̄ₚ when d≤s.
--
--   This keeps every tested root in the short-order case. In the exact-identity case it restricts the required exclusion to the local quadratic candidate set. Being in this set is only a necessary condition for interpolation; it is not asserted to supply weights. All hypotheses, witnesses, weight quantifiers and numerical bounds remain unchanged, and the geometric exclusion remains open.
-- source:
--   Derived denominator-spectrum step for https://prove2.me/theorems/35428579-0ae4-47de-bc13-8f2bb94e6f52. Mission context: Senthil Kumar K (2026), Algebraic independence of values of Weierstrass elliptic and zeta functions, Appendix A.2, Theorem A.3 and Proposition A.1; https://doi.org/10.1017/S001309152610145X. This is an algebraic tool for the interpolation frontier; the geometric zero estimate remains open. Primary Lean sources: Mathlib Algebra/Polynomial/Reverse.lean (reflect_mul, fixed-bound coefficient reflection), BigOperators.lean (product degree bounds), Degree/Defs.lean and Eval/Defs.lean, revision 0df444a360eaa60ab8c11dca51a86af692955474. See https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Polynomial/Reverse.html. Reflecting a product of quadratic denominators at the uniform bound 2*N*card(I) turns an exact rational identity into a product of characteristic quadratics vanishing at the interpolation parameter. The mission connection restricts only the sufficiently long interpolation case to this finite quadratic candidate set, preserving all witnesses and bounds.

